                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "The Gardens Between simply is a tale of friendship, exploring Arina and Frendt's relationship from the beginning of them meeting, to the conclusion.\n\nLet me start with the biggest draw of this game, and that is the variety of islands you'll visit with the friends as they make their way to the peak of each one. Visually, the game is lovely, and it's showcased very well. The islands are scattered with detailed objects that are part of Arina and Frendt's lives. An entire level dedicated to videogames and other old school tech, a level featuring a giant dinosaur display, plus many more! I thoroughly enjoyed looking around and seeing what kinds of things are strewn around the levels, plus what the overall theme of the island's was. Just a small note also; the soundtrack went along well with the overall style of the game, although I wish it wasn't always so subtle, but that's just a preference thing. \n\nThe general gameplay is fun, and although not complex, it still has enough enjoyable twists and challenge to keep things moving along nicely. The player has the ability to rewind time, and that's how you'll help Arina and Frendt navigate the islands. Like I said, it's simple, but effective! Rewinding time to put a giant dinosaur skeleton back together to move under it, and then breaking it down to help break open a block of amber keeping an item trapped is cool. Freezing time so a droplet of water conducts electricity between broken wires to open a path to move the friends forward is neat, and it was enjoyable to see how many different ways manipulating time could be used to solve puzzles. \n\nI didn't have too many complaints with this game, outside of one fairly large one. I wasn't a huge fan of how the narrative was done in The Gardens Between. It's kind of told through exploring the islands, but mostly you see it unfold in these moments after completing a series of islands where it's a scene between the two friends doing something that is supposed to show their friendship...although it falls flat for me. I didn't feel any real connection to these scenes, and I think it there was a little bit of written narrative...either through a journal or a character's thoughts, I'd feel more for them. \n\nAt the end of it all, I enjoyed my time with The Gardens Between. The islands were awesome, and the time manipulation stuff was cool, without being overly complex. It all flowed very well, and everything kept moving. No frustration, and I appreciate that a lot. This is a recommendation from me. [B]Final Rating: ⭐⭐⭐/5 (3/5). Solid, fun experience.[/B]",
